1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
[MUSIC]

2
00:00:16,617 --> 00:00:18,439
So now that we've seen Intents,

3
00:00:18,439 --> 00:00:22,350
let's talk about how you use
Intents to start activities.

4
00:00:22,350 --> 00:00:25,210
Now, if you remember
back to the last lesson,

5
00:00:25,210 --> 00:00:29,970
I talked about how you can problematically
start an activity using methods such as

6
00:00:29,970 --> 00:00:33,860
startActivity or, startActivityForResult.

7
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:36,200
Now, when you use one of these methods,

8
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:41,010
Android has to figure out which single
activity it's going to start up.

9
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:45,580
And there are two ways in
which Android does this.

10
00:00:45,580 --> 00:00:51,200
The first way is that if you explicitly
named the target activity when you created

11
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:56,930
the intent then Android can just look
that up and start that specific activity.

12
00:00:56,930 --> 00:01:00,470
The second way which is only used
if you haven't explicitly set

13
00:01:00,470 --> 00:01:02,060
the target activity,

14
00:01:02,060 --> 00:01:08,390
is that Android can implicitly determine
it based on the intent that was used and

15
00:01:08,390 --> 00:01:12,680
based on the properties of activities
that you have installed on your device.

16
00:01:14,970 --> 00:01:20,280
So let's take a look at an application
that explicitly starts another activity.

17
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:23,930
And this application is called
Hello World With Login.

18
00:01:23,930 --> 00:01:26,310
And it's comprised of two activities.

19
00:01:26,310 --> 00:01:29,280
There's one activity called LoginActivity.

20
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:32,130
And another called HelloAndroid.

21
00:01:32,130 --> 00:01:33,795
Now when this application launches,

22
00:01:33,795 --> 00:01:38,060
LoginActivity is the first
activity that's started.

23
00:01:38,060 --> 00:01:42,892
LoginActivity provides a textbox for
typing a username and another one for

24
00:01:42,892 --> 00:01:44,360
entering a password.

25
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:47,930
There is also a button and
once the user enters their username and

26
00:01:47,930 --> 00:01:51,170
password, they'll hit the Login button.

27
00:01:51,170 --> 00:01:56,350
And then LoginActivity will check
the entered pass, password and username.

28
00:01:56,350 --> 00:02:01,610
And if it accepts them, it will then
start the HelloAndroid activity,

29
00:02:01,610 --> 00:02:05,050
which will then display
the words HelloAndroid.

30
00:02:05,050 --> 00:02:05,980
Let's see that in action.

31
00:02:09,970 --> 00:02:10,980
So here's my device.

32
00:02:12,270 --> 00:02:13,420
I'll go to the home screen.

33
00:02:15,170 --> 00:02:18,930
And then I launch the HelloAndroid
with Login application.

34
00:02:20,870 --> 00:02:25,910
This pops up a user interface screen,
allowing me to enter a user name and

35
00:02:25,910 --> 00:02:26,500
a password.

36
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:29,456
Now enter that information.

37
00:02:39,304 --> 00:02:44,562
And now that I'm done,
I'll click the Login button.

38
00:02:44,562 --> 00:02:46,720
If LoginActivity accepts my user name and

39
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:53,480
password, then another activity starts
displaying the words, HelloAndroid.

40
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:56,850
So let's look at the code to see
exactly how this is implemented.

41
00:02:58,470 --> 00:03:01,490
I'll start by opening up
a LoginActivity class.

42
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:06,660
Here I'm showing a listener code
that's attached to the Login button.

43
00:03:06,660 --> 00:03:11,860
And here, the code grabs the username and
password that the user

44
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:17,195
has just entered, and then passes them
to a method called checkPassword.

45
00:03:18,342 --> 00:03:21,680
If checkPassword returns true,
then the code will continue.

46
00:03:22,910 --> 00:03:25,740
First it creates an intent
called hello intent,

47
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:33,090
and as you can see the call to the intent
constructor takes two parameters,

48
00:03:33,090 --> 00:03:38,250
a context and a class object for
the activity to be started.

49
00:03:38,250 --> 00:03:43,210
In this case LoginActivity.this
is used as the context.

50
00:03:44,620 --> 00:03:50,800
Now a context is an interface that's used
to access global application information.

51
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:54,420
And since activity is
a sub class of context,

52
00:03:54,420 --> 00:03:58,550
it's fine to use LoginActivity.this for
this parameter.

53
00:03:58,550 --> 00:04:02,610
The second parameter is the class object.

54
00:04:02,610 --> 00:04:04,765
In this case, for
the HelloAndroidActivity.

55
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:11,340
This parameter indicates the activity to
be started by the call to start activity.

56
00:04:11,340 --> 00:04:20,720
The second way to start an activity
is by implicit activation.

57
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:25,410
In this situation you haven't told
Android which activity to start so

58
00:04:25,410 --> 00:04:27,540
it has to figure it out on it's own.

59
00:04:27,540 --> 00:04:32,060
And what happens here is that Android
tries to match the intent that was

60
00:04:32,060 --> 00:04:35,170
passed in to start activity,
or start activity for

61
00:04:35,170 --> 00:04:40,400
result with the capabilities of
activities that are on your device.

62
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:43,190
And this process is
called intent resolution.

63
00:04:44,580 --> 00:04:48,440
Intent resolution relies on
two kinds of information.

64
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:52,360
First, there's the intent that
the calling activity created to

65
00:04:52,360 --> 00:04:56,430
describe the operation that
it wants to be performed.

66
00:04:56,430 --> 00:04:57,584
And second,.

67
00:04:57,584 --> 00:05:02,500
Individual Android activities
specify intent filters

68
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:07,240
which describe the kinds of operations or
intents that they can handle.

69
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:13,380
And this information is usually placed
in the AndroidManifest.XML file,

70
00:05:13,380 --> 00:05:16,910
or the applications to which
the activities belong.

71
00:05:18,510 --> 00:05:22,530
At run time when the start activity
run method is actually called,

72
00:05:22,530 --> 00:05:28,820
Android will try to match the intent with
the intent filters that it knows about.

73
00:05:28,820 --> 00:05:32,580
However, it's only going to use part
of the intent information when it

74
00:05:32,580 --> 00:05:34,370
does this matching.

75
00:05:34,370 --> 00:05:36,940
Specifically, it will
look at the Action field.

76
00:05:36,940 --> 00:05:41,920
He will look at the Data; including
both the URI and the mime type.

77
00:05:41,920 --> 00:05:43,380
And he will look at the Category.

78
00:05:45,160 --> 00:05:49,765
So let's take a look at how applications
specify IntentFilters within their

79
00:05:49,765 --> 00:05:51,939
androidmanifest.xml files.

80
00:05:53,220 --> 00:05:59,280
So here's an XML snippet showing
an activity tag and this activity tag

81
00:05:59,280 --> 00:06:04,960
includes within it an intent
filter tag,that intent filter tag

82
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:11,680
includes within it an action corresponding
to the intent action that it can support.

83
00:06:12,790 --> 00:06:13,960
For example.

84
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:17,050
If an activity can dial phone numbers,
then it

85
00:06:17,050 --> 00:06:23,610
should include an intent filter with
the standard action, intent.action_dial.

86
00:06:23,610 --> 00:06:27,040
Now that's the, the Java name for that.

87
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:29,690
The actual value is, is really a string.

88
00:06:29,690 --> 00:06:35,340
So you'll see andriod.intent.action.dial.

89
00:06:35,340 --> 00:06:38,770
That's the value that will actually
be written into the intent filter.

90
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:43,770
If your activity handles
a specific type of data,

91
00:06:43,770 --> 00:06:47,250
it can add that information to
its intent filter as well, for

92
00:06:47,250 --> 00:06:51,859
example, an activity can specify
the mime type of the data it handles.

93
00:06:52,990 --> 00:07:00,690
You can also specify the scheme, host and
or port number of the data UIs it handles.

94
00:07:00,690 --> 00:07:05,880
It can further limit the format of
the data URI by specifying it's path,

95
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:07,710
pathPattern or pathPrefix.

96
00:07:07,710 --> 00:07:13,290
And there are quite a few possibilities
here, so please see the documentation for

97
00:07:13,290 --> 00:07:14,130
more examples.

98
00:07:15,290 --> 00:07:19,210
Now, if an activity wants to
publish that it can show maps for

99
00:07:19,210 --> 00:07:23,650
example, then it might include
an intent filter, like this one,

100
00:07:23,650 --> 00:07:29,030
which tells Android that it can handle
data URIs that have a geo scheme.

101
00:07:30,380 --> 00:07:35,460
Similarly, activities can specify
categories for the intents they handle.

102
00:07:35,460 --> 00:07:38,820
And let's look at an example
that puts all of this together.

103
00:07:41,080 --> 00:07:42,830
Now, we can guess or

104
00:07:42,830 --> 00:07:46,930
imagine based on those map location
applications we saw before,

105
00:07:46,930 --> 00:07:54,790
that Google Maps can handle intents that
have an action of intent.action_view.

106
00:07:54,790 --> 00:07:58,100
And that have a data field
with the geo scheme.

107
00:07:59,720 --> 00:08:04,770
Now a while back I decided to take a look
at the Android manifest.xml file for

108
00:08:04,770 --> 00:08:06,970
the Google maps application.

109
00:08:06,970 --> 00:08:09,870
And what I saw looked something like this.

110
00:08:09,870 --> 00:08:13,170
There was an activity
with an intent filter.

111
00:08:13,170 --> 00:08:18,130
And that intent filter listed
in action of intent action.view.

112
00:08:18,130 --> 00:08:24,390
And it had data, had a data scheme of geo.

113
00:08:24,390 --> 00:08:28,670
In fact, this is exactly what the map
location applications passed into start

114
00:08:28,670 --> 00:08:34,755
activity So going back to Google maps,
the activity also listed two categories.

115
00:08:34,755 --> 00:08:39,200
Category.browsable meaning
that it can respond to

116
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:43,179
browser links and cateogry.default.

117
00:08:43,179 --> 00:08:47,480
Now it turns out that in most
cases activities that want to ac,

118
00:08:47,480 --> 00:08:53,539
accept implicit intents, must include
category.defualt in their intent filters.

119
00:08:55,878 --> 00:08:59,850
Finally when more than one activity
can accept a particular intent,

120
00:08:59,850 --> 00:09:03,190
Android will have to break
the tie in some way.

121
00:09:03,190 --> 00:09:07,100
And one way to do that is just to
ask the user which activity, he or

122
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:10,570
she wants to handle the intent and
you may have seen that.

123
00:09:10,570 --> 00:09:15,330
Another possibility is that
activities can specify a priority.

124
00:09:15,330 --> 00:09:18,940
That Android will take into
account when deciding between,

125
00:09:18,940 --> 00:09:21,260
two different activities that
can handle a particular intent.

126
00:09:21,260 --> 00:09:27,020
Now those priority values should be
between, minus 1000 and positive 1000.

127
00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:29,800
And higher values, mean higher priority.

128
00:09:32,880 --> 00:09:36,440
Now for those of you who are interested
in knowing more about intent filters,

129
00:09:36,440 --> 00:09:40,550
I recommend you take a look
at the adb dumpsys command.

130
00:09:43,830 --> 00:09:45,760
Here I've opened up a terminal window.

131
00:09:47,320 --> 00:09:56,458
And now I'll issue the command
adb shell dumpsys package.

132
00:09:56,458 --> 00:10:02,420
Now this is going to spit out a ton of
information from the package manager.

133
00:10:02,420 --> 00:10:06,330
So I'll just capture the output of
this command in to a text file.

134
00:10:06,330 --> 00:10:09,620
Let's run the command.

135
00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:12,400
And now I'll open that
file in a text editor.

136
00:10:12,400 --> 00:10:16,400
I'll use emacs and
then we'll search for the string geo.

137
00:10:18,090 --> 00:10:21,630
And what you're seeing here is that
the Google Maps application in

138
